{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773440977,"nonce":"j/t+mvU4F/yDlvbVG2B/1rDp0pM=","title":"Auth request dirty-worktree policy is inconsistent between submit and merge","message":"## Problem\n`mandible auth request` currently behaves inconsistently when the protected worktree is dirty. The submit path warns that the protected worktree is dirty, attempts an auto-fix, and can still report success even when untracked files remain. Later, the merge execution path rejects the request with a blocking message like 'Working tree is dirty ... Clean up your working tree before merging.'\n\nThis is misleading UX. The request submitter effectively says the state is acceptable or non-blocking, but the executor treats the same condition as fatal.\n\n## Context\nI hit this with an untracked repo-root `AGENTS.md` in the protected worktree. The initial auth request was accepted by the submitter with only a warning, but merge-time execution failed. That forces an unnecessary second request and makes it unclear what the real policy is.\n\nWe already have earlier fixes around untracked files and auth auto-fix behavior, but the remaining problem is policy drift: either dirty protected worktrees should be non-blocking end-to-end, or they should be blocking at submit time as well.\n\n## Desired Direction\nAdd an explicit user-configured dirty-worktree policy, with a sensible default, and make submission and execution enforce the same rule.\n\nPossible policy modes:\n- block on any dirty state\n- allow untracked-only dirtiness\n- allow auto-fixable dirtiness, block otherwise\n\nWhatever default we choose, both `mandible auth request` and merge execution should surface the same decision before the user approves the request.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}